VERDICT -- cliff
================
THE FLOOR FALLBACK IS A WORKER-COUNT PROBLEM, NOT A BUDGET PROBLEM.

ARM 2 -- worker count at 30 s, the decisive result:

      P13 (peak_util 4.09)   nw=1  87,984,880   nw=3  87,874,924   nw=4  67,982,224   nw=7  FLOOR
      P25 (peak_util 6.26)   nw=1  90,954,876   nw=3     FLOOR     nw=4     FLOOR     nw=7  FLOOR

  Seven workers floor P13; three, four and seven all floor P25.  The optimum moves with instance
  size and in opposite directions: P13 wants four, P25 wants one.

ARM 1 -- the cliff at the shipped nw=3:

              20 s        30 s        45 s        60 s        90 s
      P13    FLOOR   87,874,924  73,381,927  71,323,007  77,913,112
      P23    FLOOR   15,941,348  13,958,798  13,836,310  14,130,776
      P25    FLOOR       FLOOR   91,117,042  79,249,846  81,888,275

  All three clear by 45 s.  P25 needs 45 s at nw=3 and reaches the same value in 30 s at nw=1
  (90,954,876 against 91,117,042) -- the budget was never the binding constraint.

  P13 at 30 s with nw=4 returns 67,982,224, better than nw=3 gets at 45 s, 60 s or 90 s.  The right
  worker count is worth more than tripling the clock.

AND THE BUDGET RESPONSE IS NOT MONOTONE HERE EITHER.  P13 is worse at 90 s (77.9M) than at 60 s
(71.3M) or 45 s (73.4M); P23 is worse at 90 s than at 60 s; P25 is worse at 60 s than at 45 s.
prob_1 and prob_2 showed the same shape earlier.  More time is not reliably better anywhere in this
pipeline.

WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN ANYTHING ELSE MEASURED TODAY.  Every other arm moved the band by single
digits.  A floor answer is 4,255,755,887 against 90,954,876 -- a factor of 47 on one instance, in a
finals set of eight scored together.  And it is invisible: feas=y, no warning, no crash.

THE CPU CAP IS VINDICATED AND IS NOT SUFFICIENT.  If the grader shows all its cores, the shipped
build runs nw=7 and P13-class instances return the floor there; the cap prevents that.  But the cap
lands on nw=3, and nw=3 still floors P25 at 30 s.  What the evidence asks for is a worker count
chosen from the instance, which nwsize.log measures next.
